From d15bd8c5592daee72dc9cced335aa847bd8d1378 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 14:16:50 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 16/32] uml: read the Darwin current vCPU without pthread_once

uml_curr_cpu() is UML's smp_processor_id() on Darwin, and it is far hotter
than a per-thread lookup usually is. local_signal_state() resolves the
per-CPU signal state through it, so every block_signals()/unblock_signals()
pair reaches it -- that is Linux's local_irq_disable()/local_irq_enable(),
taken on every spinlock acquisition and every kernel entry. cpu_relax()
pays it twice per spin iteration: once directly in darwin_cpu_relax() and
once inside um_get_signals().

Every one of those calls ran pthread_once() before pthread_getspecific(),
which after initialization is pure overhead. Profiling a WebGL Aquarium
plateau with sample(1) and resolving the guest kernel against vmlinux put
uml_curr_cpu at 40% of guest-kernel CPU on the iOS Simulator and 14% on
macOS; counting the libsystem frames it calls, it is 22.3% of all vCPU
carrier CPU on the Simulator and 2.3% on macOS. A microbenchmark built for
both targets measures the shipping sequence at 4.32 ns on the Simulator and
4.56 ns on macOS against 2.13 ns and 2.18 ns for the bare getspecific, so
the once-guard is about half of it.

Publish a release-ordered ready flag from the key constructor and take an
acquire load of it on the read path instead. Every carrier creates the key
before it runs any Linux code, so a reader that observes the flag clear has
no carrier at all, and CPU0 is already the answer pthread_getspecific would
have given it. The setters keep pthread_once, so the key and its carrier
destructor are created exactly once.
---
 arch/um/os-Darwin/smp.c | 11 +++++++++--
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/um/os-Darwin/smp.c b/arch/um/os-Darwin/smp.c
index 458e885159fec443421fb2778c37b92078f36627..00a5d2f34a5585a474987fee808924b4981c6ba6 100644
--- a/arch/um/os-Darwin/smp.c
+++ b/arch/um/os-Darwin/smp.c
@@ -27,6 +27,7 @@ struct cpu_thread_data {
 
 static pthread_key_t current_cpu_key;
 static pthread_once_t current_cpu_once = PTHREAD_ONCE_INIT;
+static int current_cpu_key_ready;
 static uintptr_t cpu_threads[CONFIG_NR_CPUS];
 static uintptr_t cpu_thread_hazards[CONFIG_NR_CPUS];
 static unsigned int pending_ipis[CONFIG_NR_CPUS];
@@ -58,14 +59,20 @@ static void create_current_cpu_key(void)
 	 */
 	if (pthread_key_create(&current_cpu_key, retire_cpu_carrier) != 0)
 		panic("Darwin: unable to create current CPU key");
+	__atomic_store_n(&current_cpu_key_ready, 1, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
 }
 
 int uml_curr_cpu(void)
 {
 	struct cpu_thread_data *carrier;
 
-	if (pthread_once(&current_cpu_once, create_current_cpu_key) != 0)
-		panic("Darwin: unable to initialize current CPU key");
+	/* cpu_relax() reaches this on every spin iteration, so the read path
+	 * carries no pthread_once.  Every carrier creates the key before it runs
+	 * any Linux code, and a thread which reads before that has no carrier at
+	 * all, for which CPU0 is already the answer the key would have given.
+	 */
+	if (__atomic_load_n(&current_cpu_key_ready, __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE) == 0)
+		return 0;
 	carrier = pthread_getspecific(current_cpu_key);
 	return carrier == NULL ? 0 : carrier->cpu;
 }
